ing to  change  everything about healthcare in the  future and i would agree with you  but one of the issues that is holding  the industry back  is the lack of trained experts  according to the statistics on the  american board of medical  specialties of the more than 900 000  board certified physicians  less than 3 000 are certified  in medical genetics and genomics  according to a survey that was published  last year in genetics and medicine  from 2015 data more than 30 percent of  geneticists said that  new patients had to wait more than three  months for a non-emergency appointment  and of course that was five years ago i  can only imagine what it is today  outside of physicians of course the  need for genetic counselors is growing  uh 27 is the estimate in the next decade  and  then of course there are merging careers  that we have no data for  but just looking at job postings things  like  variant interpretation specialists  genomic scientists  etc continue to grow  so where are these genomic specialists  coming from well first i'll talk about  the phd track i am a phd i have a phd in  biology from caltech and i was trained  to do basic research  and that pipeline i would say has served  the united states extremely well  the advances in basic and applied  genomics has enabled all of this  actionability  and i will say even though there are  some threats to this system  due to the coronavirus and some of the  economic consequences  we're very good at making phds that can  make these fundamental discoveries  and so a lot of phds are going to go  into basic and applied research that  will continue to push forward the  boundaries of what we can do  thinking of getting into the clinic  however  a lot of phds do not go that route  who does go into the clinic well of  course we have medical doctors  but again that's a small percentage of  the total  md graduates in the united states end up  working in genetics  an increasing number of nurse  practitioners are specializing in  genetics  and of course we have genetic counselors  i came into this world through genetic  counseling  kgi decided to start a genetic  counseling program  to address the profound shortage in  genetic counselors  especially on the west coast of the  united states and  i was recruited to help teach genetics  and genomics in this program  and it really got me interested in this  field of genetic counseling  which i admit i knew very little about  at the time  and one of the things that makes it an  exciting career choice is that you don't  have to go into clinical practice there  are roles for genetic counselors in the  genomics industry  at testing companies and development of  new tests and in drug development  but that means that a large percentage  of people every year who graduate with a  genetic counseling degree  don't end up working in clinical  practice for their entire careers  which means the pipeline is further  narrowed  we also have people who contribute to  this industry with other  graduate degrees like bioinformatics  also have biostatistics  but again these experts are not going  into clinical practice  so how do we change the balance of these  arrows not  by reducing who is going into the  genomics industry  or the drug development path but by  expanding the total number of people  going into applied genomics  i'll start by talking a little bit about  the institution i'm a part of and that's  kgi i assume that a lot of you have  never heard of us  um we're very small and we're relatively  young in the higher education space  founded just in 1997 kgi was however  founded specifically  for the applied life sciences so we're a  graduate level  institution and all of our programs are  either health science or life science  but we do try to be very responsive to  industry  and to healthcare settings we're the  youngest of  the seven claremont colleges in  claremont california and you see a  lovely picture here of our campus and  the mountains t