uch work in that  field right now i think just the way  society has embraced  genetic testing and the power of  genomics  a lot of young people already want to go  into  precision medicine and so the only thing  we need to do is expose them to all of  the careers  that are involved in that if they don't  want to go to medical school  how can they play a role in this  exciting part of  health science and of course some are  immersion programs to get  students the expertise they'll need to  get into undergraduate programs which  will then  build on their talents in the  undergraduate sphere  we do have a summer immersion program at  kgi of course this summer it will not be  in person  but again we also want to expose  undergrads to  careers in genomics that maybe aren't as  traditional  as being a doctor or a nurse  very few undergraduates have ever heard  of genetic counseling or even  the genomics industry and so giving them  the opportunity to just see the options  is one part of providing students for  the pipeline  now of course kgi is a graduate  institution so we do award master's  degrees  and we have two of these programs the  gen ed counseling program and the  genomic data analytics program  that provide clinicians and  support services to the genomics  industry  but we also know a lot of people are  going to get into this pipeline  through a traditional medical school  experience  and it's important that we now integrate  genetics and genomics  into medical school education so it  comes as second nature  when a doctor is handed a genetics  report  the biggest audience however though are  the people that are already there  and these are people who are working  clinicians either genetic counselors  nurses doctors that want more genetics  and genomics expertise  including people who work in industry  and want to better serve  the precision medicine workforce and so  we will address this with executive  education programs  and continuing education workshops just  building  that expertise at all levels  and so that is the full spectrum  approach  so what kind of skills do we want an  an ideal applied genomics expert  well when we set out to design our  programs i did a lot of interviews  with people working in the industry side  and clinical sites  in genomics testing companies just to  get a handle on what  their perfect new employee would be  and we kept coming back to these three  core domains  the first is just genetics knowledge  that  is why genetic counselors can get jobs  all throughout the industries because  people are very interested  in experts that know genetics through  and through understanding  genomics now with all the fundamental  insights we've learned in the last few  years  as an appreciation for human variation  that is up to date on what we know on  structural variation  on common variation on rare variants  and again on the impact of ancestry  and our current limitations and  data from multi-ethnic cohorts  the next big skill set  was in data science so these are  hands-on skills the ability to have some  knowledge of command line programming  to be able to do data analytics and to  also  work in this space managing files in a  hipaa compliant way  of course in genomics we have very large  files sometimes  that we want to stay with the patient so  really understanding how you manage this  information on a practical  level and the last domain is something  that comes up  at kgi in all of our programs  and that's just what it takes to be an  effective co-worker  in the current state of  the economy and that is someone who has  great communication skills  who understands how to work in a team  has strong ethics  and all of the skills that go along with  being  a professional  so at kgi we decided to address these  three  core areas with a new program a master  of human genetics and genomic data  analytics  and i always point out to our students  that the first part is the master of  human genetics  it 