one in our program  takes  these courses in the first year we're  very heavy in just the basics  of genetics and  analytical skills so in the first  semester the students will be taking  a full course in human molecular  genetics and genetic disease mechanisms  but they'll also be taking programming  for the biosciences which is a class  taught in python we also do teach just  the basics of working in the command  line  and connecting to remote servers other  skills  that will help them use programs built  by others  in the second semester they get an  entire semester of human genomics  that's coupled with a human genomics lab  uh we do have uh midnight on sequencer  so we do long read sequencing  we use publicly available data to do  a trio analysis with short read  sequencing  they get expertise going from fast queue  files all the way to variant call  files and then interpreting variants  using the acmg guidelines  they also have courses in clinical  cancer genomics medical genetics and  biochemical genetics and that really  is the foundation of all of their  knowledge they will take forward  at the same time they're doing those  courses in the first year they're also  doing their applied life science  curriculum  so they do take a course in medical  diagnostics  to really understand where this data  comes from  they take the introduction to bioscience  industry to understand  how this industry works and what their  role will be  they do take clinical biostats and  healthcare ethics  and they also get the opportunity to  take a biomedical data systems and  informatics class  which also includes transcriptomics and  this is of course taught in the  programming language r  and they take a clinical trial design  and literature evaluation course to  start learning how to read clinical  literature as opposed to just  basic science literature so in the  first year curriculum we really give  them a strong foundation and they take  that into their summer internship  and 