hat are nearby  we're divided into two schools the henry  e riggs school of applied life sciences  houses the original programs and that's  our phd in applied life science the  master of business and science  masters of engineering and bioprocessing  and then  other master of science programs and our  certificate programs  we also have a school of pharmacy and  health sciences  that was built in the last few years for  an innovative pharmd curriculum  and once the pharmd program was up and  running  the leadership decided to look into  genetic counseling  and that's when i came to kgi to help  with the  establishment of our genetics programs  to teach  human genetics and human genomics and in  the process of starting the genetic  counseling  program the leadership had this crazy  idea to also start a companion program  in genomic data analytics to really be  out there meeting the need of the jobs  of the future and i'll talk about how we  structure that  unless we have an occupational therapy  program coming soon  some of you may know that we are also in  the process of starting the school of  medicine  it'll be very exciting to see how that  develops in the next couple years  one thing that's really important to  know about kgi is that  innovation is our motto  so innovators start here is the tagline  on all of our  banners and campus materials  and it really is um  understanding at kgi that we really want  to be  near the cutting edge and to create new  models in education  so how do we do this in the space of  genomics  well we wanted to take a full spectrum  approach  there is the traditional way at kga of  course we have these two masters  programs  and so we can admit small cohorts of  students  and get them graduate degrees but that's  not the only way we can expand the  pipeline  so starting on the left side of the  screen we have  initiatives to help increase  interest in high school now i made this  arrow a little bit smaller because we  don't have to do much work in that  field right now i think just the way  society has embraced  genetic testing and the power of  genomics  a lot of young people already want to go  into  precision medicine and so the only thing  we need to do is expose them to all of  the careers  that are involved in that if they don't  want to go to medical school  how can they play a role in this  exciting part of  health science and of course some are  immersion programs to get  students the expertise they'll need to  get into undergraduate programs which  will then  build on their talents in the  undergraduate sphere  we do have a summer immersion program at  kgi of course this summer it will not be  in person  but again we also want to expose  undergrads to  careers in genomics that maybe aren't as  traditional  as being a doctor or a nurse  very few undergraduates have ever heard  of genetic counseling or even  the genomics industry and so giving them  the opportunity to just see the options  is one part of providing students for  the pipeline  now of course kgi is a graduate  institution so we do award master's  degrees  and we have two of these programs the  gen ed counseling program and the  genomic data analytics program  that provide clinicians and  support services to the genomics  industry  but we also know a lot of people are  going to get into this pipeline  through a traditional medical school  experience  and it's important that we now integrate  genetics and genomics  into medical school education so it  comes as second nature  when a doctor is handed a genetics  report  the biggest audience however though are  the people that are already there  and these are people who are working  clinicians either genetic counselors  nurses doctors that want more genetics  and genomics expertise  including people who work in industry  and want to better serve  the precision medicine workforce and so  we will address this with executive  education programs  and continuing education workshops just  building  that expertise at all levels  and so that is the full spectrum  approach  so what kind of skills do we want an  an ideal applied genomics expert  well when we set out to design our  programs i did a lot of interviews  with people working in the industry side  and clinical sites  in genomics testing companies just to  get a handle on what  their perfect new employee would be  and we kept coming back to these three  core domains  the first is just genetics knowledge  that  is why genetic counselors can get jobs  all throughout the industries because  people are very interested  in experts that know genetics through  and through understanding  genomics now with all the fundamental  insights we've learned in the last few  years  as an appreciation for human variation  that is up to date on what we know on  structural variation  on common variation on rare variants  and again on the impact of ancestry  and our current limitations and  data from multi-ethnic cohorts  the next big skill set  was in data science so these are  hands-on skills the ability to have some  knowledge of command line programming  to be able to do data analytics and to  also  work in this space managing files in a  hipaa compliant way  of course in genomics we have very large  files sometimes  that we want to stay with the patient so  really understanding how you manage this  information on a practical  level and the last domain is something  that comes up  at kgi in all of our programs  and that's just what it takes to be an  effective co-worker  in the current state of  the economy and that is someone who has  great communication skills  who understands how to work in a team  has strong ethics  and all of the skills that go along with  being  a professional  so at kgi we decided to address these  three  core areas with a new program a master  of human genetics and genomic data  analytics  and i always point out to our students  that the first part is the master of  human genetics  it is a master of science and we really  want the students to fundamentally  understand  human genetics we also want them to  understand  the applied life sciences and really  benefit from kgi's long track record in  that space  and to have this innovative human  genomics curriculum  that is on the leading edge trying to  figure out what is going to be important  in five to ten years when they're still  in practice  that we're only learning about today  a highlight of this program is the  ability to work side by side with our  future genetic counselors and our  applied life science students as  students excuse me well they're gaining  all of this hands-on experience  but they're also building up their  professional network and really  understanding  the roles and needs of people they will  interact with but are in different  professions  and last we want to make sure our  graduates have this foundation in  genetics and genomics but  have an ability to translate that to an  actionable clinical recommendation  and really be the person that's the  between the research scientist  and the clinical practitioner  so how did we structure this program  well it is a 20-month 21-month two-year  program  we have our core genetics courses that  they take alongside the genetic  counseling students  then we have the applied life science  courses that are  based on previous kgi offerings and we  have a summer internship where  the students get hands-on practice  doing all of these skills and then in  the second year we have specialty  courses in genomic data analytics  and the students have the opportunity to  pick a concentration  our three concentrations are clinical  decision support  clinical trial design and assay  development  and last the students do a capstone  project in collaboration with an outside  either medical center or  research institute or industry site to  develop a showcase of their skills  these are the courses that we put  together for our  two-year program everyone in our program  takes  these courses in the first year we're  very heavy in just the basics  of genetics and  analytical skills so in the first  semester the students will be taking  a full course in human molecular  genetics and genetic disease mechanisms  but they'll also be taking programming  for the biosciences which is a class  taught in python we also do teach just  the basics of working in the command  line  and connecting to remote servers other  skills  that will help them use programs built  by others  in the second semester they get an  entire semester of human genomics  that's coupled with a human genomics lab  uh we do have uh midnight on sequencer  so we do long read sequencing  we use publicly available data to do  a trio analysis with short read  sequencing  they get expertise going from fast queue  files all the way to variant call  files and then interpreting variants  using the acmg guidelines  they also have courses in clinical  cancer genomics medical genetics and  biochemical genetics and that really  is the foundation of all of their  knowledge they will take forward  at the same time they're doing those  courses in the first year they're also  doing their applied life science  curriculum  so they do take a course in medical  diagnostics  to really understand where this data  comes from  they take the introduction to bioscience  industry to understand  how this industry works and what their  role will be  they do take clinical biostats and  healthcare ethics  and they also get the opportunity to  take a biomedical data systems and  informatics class  which also includes transcriptomics and  this is of course taught in the  programming language r  and they take a clinical trial design  and literature evaluation course to  start learning how to read clinical  literature as opposed to just  basic science literature so in the  first year curriculum we really give  them a strong foundation and they take  that into their summer internship  and then they come back the second year  and a lot of their time is spent on  their capstone project  six units each semester but they also  have the second year curriculum  that we've designed specifically for  roles that we think are  very important in the future and the  first is a full semester in dna  sequencing and variant analysis  really teaching the students how to use  the  clinical guidelines to understand  variants that they find  in patients we also have a course in  functional genomics  where they learn a lot of the research  side  techniques including chromatin  confirmation capture  and other things that i think are fun  but the students  might interact with later if these  become useful  to the clinical space  we also have a course that is called  genomic knowledge translation and this  is really a communications class  because we have spent two years filling  their heads with lots of very technical  knowledge  they then need to know how to give this  information back to a variety of  audiences  in a way that is digestible so we do  have a course  that does talk about presenting their  information  in a variety of formats unless we have a  genomic data visualization and  management course  and this also reinforces their skills  in file management and programming so  this course  uses both r and python and really  sets them up for working in a hipaa  compliant way  now we have another  course in pharmacogenomics and advanced  pharmaceutical discovery that we do in  the second year  and this really leverages the fact that  the students are  in the school of pharmacy and a lot of  them are interested in how  genomics is changing drug development  and  this course really highlights the  um changes in our field there  now i did mention each student does get  the opportunity to pick a concentration  we have three to choose from the first  concentration was  clinical decision support this is really  how we built the program  thinking about the ideal education to be  a variant curator  and for those students who really want  to go that route  we picked from our other existing  courses at kgi  to offer molecular basis of disease  medical terminology advanced writing  we do have some fundamental papers  classes applied medicine  but i'll highlight two courses in this  track and the first is our human  embryology and prenatal diagnosis  this course is required for our genetic  counseling students but is not required  for the genomic data analytics students  but a lot of our gda students felt that  it was very useful for understanding  um genetics and how this plays out in  human beings and so that is a very  popular elective  the other course i wanted to point out  was our machine learning and the life  sciences course  and this is for our students who have  the programming skills and interest to  really  learn how machine learning can help  automate  in the future a lot of the things we do  by hand today  and i think it'll be an incredible skill  set to bring to any  position where we're increasingly seeing  ai  help speed along workflows  and do first pass interpretation  when we were building the msgda  degree we did get a lot of input from  one of our partners at amgen and  they were telling us how important  genetics now is in the clinical trial  development aspect of the pharmaceutical  industry  and the need for trained genetics  professionals in that space  and so for people interested in going  into drug development  and pharmacogenomics we put together  these courses from  our existing offerings first of course  is the introduction to food and drug law  i think the fda  policies for genetic testing and test  development have never been  in the news more so i anticipate this  course is going to be very popular in  the fall  but these issues are important to know  if you plan on developing  the next great pharmaceutical or  even a genetic test then kgi does have  our courses in pharmaceutical discovery  and development  and design of clinical trials  the last uh concentration that we came  up with was assay development these are  for our true  entrepreneurs and there's a very  entrepreneurial spirit at kgi  and so for those students who have taken  all this information and have a great  idea for a new product  we wanted them to know what it would  take to bring it to market  and so this puts together some of our  business and science courses  including the food and drug law and our  advanced in vitro diagnostics business  operations  and again some of the fundamental papers  just to understand what is actually  needed in the field  so these are our three concentrations i  will say most of our students so far  have chosen the clinical decision  support pathway  and for those working in precision  medicine i think this  is the pathway that best um  captures the skills knowledge and  expertise  that will be needed to be an effective  precision medicine specialist  now the heart of our program is actually  what happens when the students are off  campus  and that's the summer experience because  i think  nothing helps the students really  understand what all of this didactic  coursework means  than seeing it put into practice so all  of our students complete a summer  internship at either a clinical genomics  lab  a company a research center  below you see the place where our first  cohort spent their summer  so we had students at labcorp by marin  st  jude scripps translational merck and the  nih  and this experience really gave all of  our students  an insight into what skills were really  used day-to-day  and why they needed to know all those  things that i told them that they needed  to know  and we hope that the summer experience  translates into their capstone project  as i said in the second year the  students spend 18 hours a week working  on a project  and if everything goes to plan they will  just extend their  summer internship project however we do  have some places where this is not  possible  or they get really interested in  something else  so it's not a requirement but  we also have these capstone projects  that allow our students to work  at sites like these to do a more  long-term  and in-depth project so i ask all of you  listening if you have  a project that you've always wanted to  work on and  just don't have enough time for it um  think whether one of our students might  be able to help you in either the summer  or in the school year to really make it  into an interesting and useful product  and project and base of knowledge for  the rest of the fields by  having a project for one of our students  so so far i've talked to you about this  program as if it is still taking place  at kgi  um we had our first cohort came on  campus in the fall of 2018  and they were scheduled to graduate  next month unfortunately they are not  going to be able to have their  commencement  in may as it was planned because we have  gone completely online  all of these programs were designed in  person  with the summer internship experience  but you know we always talked about well  what if the  second year was online then they could  work anywhere in the united states they  wouldn't have to work in southern  california  they could maybe telecommute to sites  all over the country and there was a  general feel that  sometime in the future we would put  portions of this  online of course one week  before spring break we were told that we  would not be coming back to campus due  to the coronavirus  and so we had nine days to convert  our entire curriculum to online  education and i will  say that i have been extremely thankful  for the  grace of our students that they have  made it work and our  faculty have made it work and so right  now we are fully online  but i'll put an asterisk because this is  fully online with  a temporary feel to it we're using zoom  we're doing the best we can but it's  still based on what we would have done  in a classroom and we are just sort of  doing it  remotely in the future  of course we can do this a lot better  and there are  um hybrid and fully online programs that  really leverage  all of the capacity of  the internet and remote computing  to do really cool things with the  students not sitting in the same room  and so i think just in the future i  don't know that we're ever going back  to um an institution where the majority  of our  classes meet in physical spaces now this  is  both exciting and scary but i think  all across the the world we're trying to  figure out how we want to do  education if there can be times when  we're told  with very little notice you can no  longer come to this physical location  and this brings up the question of  timing so right now all of our classes  are synchronous so we meet for a  specific time i teach human genomics  right now  from 10 30 to noon on mondays and  wednesdays and all of the students have  to come together  at that time to participate in the class  of course there is a model that is more  asynchronous  at kgi we do sometimes use the flipped  classroom  so for my human molecular genetics  course that of course all of the  lectures  are pre-recorded and so students can  watch those lectures whenever they  would like and we only meet to do  hands-on learning activities now  in an asynchronous model the students  would be working as a group but they  would not necessarily come together at  the same time  they would collaborate but not um  in person together  there are some advantages to the  asynchronous style of course  if you are a working clinician or if you  have a family it may be very beneficial  to schedule  uh your education around other things  that you cannot change  however the synchronous model does have  the ability for people to come together  and really do that group work  and have a chance to discuss with  faculty members and have those  spontaneous conversations  that really lead to  increased discovery and also enthusiasm  for their education  of course the programs i've talked about  are full-time programs they're very  heavy  intense two-year programs but we do need  to think about what we're doing for  certificate-based programs or just  continuing education  things that can be delivered in small  chunks  and of course we do want to do this with  very forward thinking pedagogy  which is team based learning  problem-based learning everything very  active  um and this means not just listening to  uh me lecture i know i love to lecture  because  it is my easiest way to  take all of the things in my head and  say them at the students  but i know for a fact that they don't  learn that way and so  the ability to do things that actually  challenge assumptions  make people circle back make them use  these skills  until they become second nature that is  how we're going to have to do  education and that includes people  who have not perhaps learned in this way  before  i know um through some of my  conversations with  colleagues that if you went to  medical school say 20 years ago you're  not  used to team-based active learning  flipped classroom style education so it  may be very hard to transition  learning styles but i think if we do it  the right way  it can be something that's very valuable  to people at all spectrum of their  career  and the last is to get people into the  setting where they're actually going to  work  either through internships during gaps  in the curriculum or through co-ops  where they're working in the field  getting hands-on expertise  while also getting didactic  or classroom learning to build upon what  they're seeing  at their site we are very interested in  developing more of these  partnerships so if you or someone you  know might have  a site that really wants the energy of  having students  and the ability to have someone go on a  deep dive on a project we would love to  talk to you  and so to address some of these issues  um kg i was very fortunate to  get a gift from amgen last year to build  out a new  center and this center is called  the center for training and applied  genomics the ctag  and it's really going to allow kgi to  take this full spectrum  approach where we have young people who  are interested in going into genomics  whether they're  undergraduates or high school students  evening  and given giving them master's programs  or  even phd programs and other programs to  get them into the field  as well as executive education  and continuing education for people who  are already in the field and just want  to catch up  i feel like if i blink i miss a year's  worth of genomics  discoveries and so having programming  that will keep people up to date when  they're very busy in their clinic  already  i think will be um something we can add  to this field  and so our current offerings right now  are the master of science in genetic  counseling  and i didn't talk about that program and  what makes that program very unique  today but you can get more information  on our website  the masters of science and genomic data  analytics  and we're launching this year our first  summer program in clinical genetics and  bioinformatics  we had planned a nice on-campus  two-week fully in-depth hands-on program  but unfortunately this will be  a remote online offering this summer  but it does give us the opportunity to  open it up to so many more students this  way  so we will still be running our first  summer program  in clinical genetics and bioinformatics  and of course we have  lots of other ideas that are percolating  through  and please stay tuned for any other  exciting programs we announce  so in summary genomics is changing and  it's changing  extremely fast so  how do we change education to keep up  with it and if we're going to change  healthcare we're going to have to change  education first  we also need to look at what are the  barriers to making precision medicine  the standard of care  and that is we need an expanded  workforce with training in genetics  genomics data analysis  and scientific communication and to  fully realize this potential  we have to take the full spectrum  approach we have to build the people  interested  and we have to take people working in  the clinic and also give them those  skills  that they need right now so thank you  very much for  listening and i appreciate any questions  through the chat provided by
 

